# Fumola, Local and Remote #

# Three ideas, one per slide after this one. #

# 1. LOCAL. A Fumola instance lives in the page. ^fumola_wip sends #
#    it a program; Fumola's runtime, compiled to WebAssembly, runs it #
#    in the browser; the result comes back as a Hazel value. #

# 2. REMOTE. The same instance can live on the Internet Computer #
#    instead. The program is sent to a canister as mobile code, run #
#    there by the same runtime (one Rust crate, fumola_wasm_common, #
#    linked into both), and the result is sent back in the same JSON, #
#    so it becomes a Hazel value in exactly the same way. #

# 3. SHARED. The canister also keeps Hazel's state, in named spaces. #
#    A page opts some of its keys into a space; every page that names #
#    the same space reads and writes the same keys. That is the start #
#    of slides anyone can add and everyone can see. #

# What stays the same across all three is the point: one language, #
# one runtime, one value boundary. Only where the computation and #
# the storage live changes. #

# Local first: everything here runs against a local replica. The #
# last slide is the plan for mainnet. #
()
